@*
    The bar a surface puts up once a reader has picked more than one of
    something: how many, a way back out, a box that takes the rest, and whatever
    the host can do to them all at once.

    Chrome and nothing else. The bar knows a count and a total; it does not know
    what was selected, what the acts are, or what any of them would do — that is
    the Actions slot, exactly as `TaskItem.Actions` is. One implementation of
    "3 items selected" therefore serves every list in the product rather than
    one per list, which is the whole reason it is here rather than in a pane.

    `.design/interaction-guidelines.md#focus-and-selection` writes the rule down:
    a live count, a clear-selection control, and a select-all that shows the
    indeterminate state on a partial selection. All three are structural, so all
    three are drawn here rather than left to a host to remember.

    The count is a live region because it changes without the reader having
    touched it — a shift-click takes eight rows, and a number that changed
    silently is a number only somebody watching it can read. It is in the DOM from
    the first render for the same reason the task list's move announcement is: a
    live region has to exist before it changes, or the first change is the one
    nobody hears.

    Checkbox, not a checkbox. `Inputs/Checkbox.razor` already carries the
    tri-state as `aria-checked="mixed"` where assistive technology reads it; a
    second box here would be a second place to get the one thing this bar is
    specifically required to get right.

    AppButton for the clear, for the same reason: a ghost button at small size is
    what a quiet control on a bar looks like everywhere else in the product, and
    the bar has no argument for a different one.

    The noun is the host's. "3 items selected" is what a component with no
    vocabulary can honestly say, and a list of entries wants "tasks" — so the
    singular is a parameter and the plural is derived from it unless a host has an
    irregular one to hand over.
*@

<div @attributes="AdditionalAttributes" class="@ClassValue" role="group" aria-label="@AriaLabel" data-testid="@TestId">
    <Checkbox Checked="@AllSelected"
              Indeterminate="@Indeterminate"
              CheckedChanged="SelectAllChanged"
              Disabled="@(Total == 0)"
              CssClass="selection-bar__all"
              TestId="@Suffix("select-all")"
              aria-label="@SelectAllLabel"
              title="@SelectAllLabel" />

    <span class="selection-bar__count"
          role="status"
          aria-live="polite"
          data-testid="@Suffix("count")">@CountText</span>

    @if (Actions is not null)
    {
        @* A group of its own, so a host's acts sit together between the count and
           the way out rather than being flexed apart from each other. Named, for
           the reason every other group in this library is: a reader tabbing into
           a row of controls should hear what the row is for. *@
        <span class="selection-bar__actions"
              role="group"
              aria-label="@ActionsLabel"
              data-testid="@Suffix("actions")">
            @Actions
        </span>
    }

    @* Last, and deliberately: everything to the left of it acts on the selection
       and this one ends it. The same order the task row puts its bin in. *@
    <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
               Size="ButtonSize.Small"
               CssClass="selection-bar__clear"
               TestId="@Suffix("clear")"
               OnClick="OnClear">
        @ClearLabel
    </AppButton>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>How many things are selected. The one number the bar is about.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public int Count { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// How many there are to select — the rows the reader can currently see, not
    /// everything the host holds.
    /// <para>
    /// It is here because the select-all box cannot be honest without it: "all"
    /// and "some" are the same count until something says what the whole is, and
    /// a bar that took <c>AllSelected</c> and <c>Indeterminate</c> as separate
    /// flags would let a host hand over a pair that contradict each other.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public int Total { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised by the leading box: true for "take the rest", false for
    /// "let them all go". The bar never decides which rows those are.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<bool> SelectAllChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised by the way out. Always offered: a reader who can get into
    /// a selection has to be able to get out of one without hunting for the row
    /// they started on.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnClear { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the host can do to everything selected, handed the room to
    /// draw it in. Unfilled, the bar draws no group at all — an element with an
    /// aria-label about nothing is worse than no element.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Actions { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What one of the selected things is called, in the singular.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string ItemNoun { get; set; } = "item";

    /// <summary>What several of them are called, when an <c>-s</c> will not do.
    /// Unset, the plural is derived, which is right for every noun this product
    /// actually counts.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? ItemNounPlural { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string SelectAllLabel { get; set; } = "Select all";

    [Parameter]
    public string ClearLabel { get; set; } = "Clear selection";

    [Parameter]
    public string AriaLabel { get; set; } = "Selection";

    [Parameter]
    public string ActionsLabel { get; set; } = "Apply to selection";

    /// <summary>Replaces <c>selection-bar</c>, for a host that already dresses a
    /// bar of its own.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string BaseClass { get; set; } = "selection-bar";

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Splatted onto the bar itself, for the attribute or the handler a host needs
    /// on the whole of it rather than on one control in it.
    /// <para>
    /// Splatted <em>first</em>, so the component wins every attribute it names.
    /// The class and the role are the two that matter and the two a host is most
    /// likely to reach for: a <c>class</c> here would erase <c>selection-bar</c>
    /// and every layout rule keyed on it, and a <c>role="toolbar"</c> would
    /// replace the <c>group</c> that <see cref="ActionsLabel"/> and
    /// <see cref="AriaLabel"/> are both written against. A host that wants to add
    /// to the class has <see cref="CssClass"/>, and one that wants to replace it
    /// has <see cref="BaseClass"/> — two hooks that say what they do, where a
    /// splat that happened to land last would have said nothing and worked once.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The case it exists for is a key. A bar is the surface a reader is standing
    /// on while a selection is live, so "Escape means put this down" is a rule
    /// about the bar and not about any single button on it — and a wrapper element
    /// carrying the listener is not available to a host here: the bar is a sticky
    /// child of its list's scroll container, and sticky can only move within its
    /// own parent's box, so a div wrapped around it would be a box exactly the
    /// bar's height and the bar would stop sticking.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Everything is selected only when there is something to select.
    /// Zero of zero is an empty list rather than a full one, and a box that read
    /// as checked over nothing would offer to clear a selection nobody made.</summary>
    private bool AllSelected => Total > 0 && Count >= Total;

    private bool Indeterminate => Count > 0 && Count < Total;

    private string CountText =>
        $"{Count} {(Count == 1 ? ItemNoun : ItemNounPlural ?? ItemNoun + "s")} selected";

    private string ClassValue => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass)
        ? BaseClass
        : $"{BaseClass} {CssClass}";

    private string? Suffix(string part) => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-{part}";
}
